## Verdict

SOLVED: maybe

I think there is now a credible exact characterization for the finite-horizon exogenous minimax model, but it is not a tail-only characterization. The right object is a least-favorable Bayes predictability gap. The original \(\beta(h)\to0\) condition is only a sufficient tail condition.

## Candidate Solution Or Main Attempt

Assume finite horizon \(T\), public exogenous prices, learner knows the class \(\mathcal C_T=\{P^\theta:\theta\in\Theta_T\}\) but not \(\theta\), actions do not affect prices, and the learner may depend on \(T\).

Let
\[
M_t=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s,\qquad r_t(a)=a{\bf 1}\{M_t>a\},
\]
and
\[
V_\theta(T)=\sup_{a\in[0,1]}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbb E_\theta r_t(a).
\]

For a prior \(\pi\) over \(\Theta_T\), let \(\bar P_\pi\) be the mixture law. Define the Bayes predictability gap
\[
\Psi_T(\pi)
=
\mathbb E_{\theta\sim\pi}V_\theta(T)
-
\sum_{t=1}^T
\mathbb E_{\bar P_\pi}
\left[
\sup_{a\in[0,1]}
a\,\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t})
\right].
\]

Claim: for finite model classes and finite action grids, the minimax regret equals
\[
\sup_\pi \Psi_T(\pi).
\]
For the continuum action set, this should hold under standard measurable-selection/minimax regularity, or up to the usual \(T/K\) grid error.

Thus the information-theoretic characterization is:

\[
\text{sublinear minimax regret is possible}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\left(\sup_\pi \Psi_T(\pi)\right)_+=o(T).
\]

This explains all known examples. Degenerate known-law priors have no positive obstruction because the learner can use posterior predictive maximization. Hidden-terminal two-world priors have a positive Bayes gap because the active reward curve is not identifiable before many decisions.

The practical sufficient theorem remains:

\[
\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_T}R_T^\theta
\lesssim
\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}+T/K+\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_T}\delta_T^\theta(H),
\]
where
\[
\delta_T^\theta(H)
=
V_\theta(T)
-
\sup_a\sum_t\mathbb E_\theta r_t^H(a).
\]
Since \(\delta_T^\theta(H)\le \Delta_T^\theta(H)\le T\beta_\theta(H)\), the original \(\beta\)-condition gives sublinear regret but is not necessary.

For \(\beta(h)\lesssim h^{-\alpha}\), this upper route gives
\[
R_T\lesssim
T^{(\alpha+1)/(2\alpha+1)}(\log T)^{\alpha/(2\alpha+1)}
\]
with \(K=T\).

## Concrete Lemmas Or Reductions

1. Known-law oracle: for every known finite-horizon exogenous law, conditional grid maximization gives \(R_T\le T/K\), hence \(O(1)\) with \(K=T\).

2. Bayes myopic optimality: for a fixed prior \(\pi\), actions do not affect future observations, so the Bayes-optimal learner maximizes
\[
a\,\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t})
\]
separately at each time \(t\). There is no exploration value.

3. Least-favorable prior reduction: under minimax equality, the minimax value is the supremum over priors of the Bayes regret \(\Psi_T(\pi)\).

4. Truncated full-information reduction: with \(H\)-truncated rewards, use \(H+1\) residue-class Hedge instances on a \(K\)-grid to get
\[
O(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}).
\]

5. Hidden-terminal atom: two indistinguishable worlds for \(D\) rounds, with terminal distributions on \([1/2,5/8]\) and \([3/4,7/8]\), force regret \(D/8\). The \(q\)-scaled version forces \(qD/8\).

## Gaps And Failure Points

The exact Bayes-gap characterization still needs a clean minimax theorem statement for general law classes and continuum actions. A finite-class, finite-grid version is safe.

This is not a satisfying scalar tail characterization. It is essentially an information-value characterization: valuable delayed rewards matter only when the learner cannot predict which reward curve is active.

The power-tail lower bound is still not matched. The \(q\)-scaled one-block atom gives only \(\Omega(DB(D))\), too weak for \(B(h)=h^{-\alpha}\).

## Counterexamples Or Obstructions

Raw \(\beta\) necessity is false: iid uniform prices have \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but a known-law learner can match the fixed comparator.

\(\Delta_T(H)=o(T)\) necessity is false: a known terminal-spike law has linear truncation loss but zero regret for a law-aware learner.

Same tail, different minimax behavior: a singleton \(q\)-terminal law is learnable when known; the two-world hidden-terminal class with comparable tail has regret \(\Omega(qD)\). Tail alone misses indistinguishability.

Action-dependent prices remain outside the reduction; counterfactual reward vectors are not fixed unless prices are exogenous.

## Promising Ideas To Explore

Formalize the finite-grid minimax/Bayes theorem first, then pass to continuum via downward rounding.

Use \(\Psi_T(\pi)\) as the lower-bound language in the paper: it cleanly contains the hidden-terminal examples and rules out false tail iff claims.

For matching rates, pursue multi-scale or limited-leakage priors. The construction must accumulate Bayes gap across scales without iid block-splicing collapsing the fixed comparator.

## Notes For Critics

Check the sign convention: \(\Psi_T(\pi)\) can be negative because adaptive learners may beat the fixed comparator. The obstruction is its positive least-favorable part.

Check whether the posterior predictive term should condition only on \(P_{1:t}\) or also on learner private randomness. Under exogenous prices, private randomness carries no information about \(\theta\), so \(P_{1:t}\) should suffice.

Do not promote the Bayes-gap characterization as a clean tail theorem. It is exact-looking, but it says the answer is about predictability plus value, not raw delay.
